      "id": 1,
      "text": "Kenyon Riddle named the lake after Seminole chief Yaholoochee, known as “The Cloud,” honoring a local war hero.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://cloudlakefl.us/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Removing fill for the Seaboard Air Line Railroad overpass created a five‑acre lake that now defines Cloud Lake’s shoreline.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://cloudlakefl.us/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "On February 17, 1923, the community dedicated the Veteran’s Memorial Library, a cornerstone of local heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Cloud%2C_Florida",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In its early years, Cloud Lake maintained a sundown policy, reserving a “Colored Quarter” for black residents outside town limits.",
